course: dictionary
lesson: 10572
title: Words for behaviour: uncouth
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Uncouth is a manners word. It describes how someone behaves, such as burping loudly, talking with a full mouth or shouting. «Toad would crouch on the front chair, making uncouth and ghastly noises.» Ghastly means horrible, so uncouth must describe rude, rough behaviour.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Toad is making a racket and acting wild, not politely. Now try your meaning in the sentence: «making rude and rough noises» still makes sense, so uncouth is the right meaning.
explain: Uncouth is about behaviour, not money. A rich person can be uncouth, and a poor person can have lovely manners. In The Wind in the Willows, the weasels and stoats are uncouth guests in Toad Hall because of how they act.
fact: Uncouth means rude, rough and without good manners.
fact: «Uncouth» describes behaviour, such as burping loudly, talking with your mouth full or shouting.
fact: «Uncouth» is about manners, not money: a wealthy person can be uncouth.
fact: «Ghastly» means horrible or frightful, so «uncouth and ghastly noises» are rough, horrible sounds.
fact: Words with a similar meaning to uncouth include rude and rough. The opposite is polite or well-mannered.
exercise: «Toad crouched on the chair, making uncouth and ghastly noises.» What does uncouth mean? | *Rude and rough, without good manners | Very tidy and neat | Quiet and shy | Sleepy and slow
exercise: A guest eats with his mouth wide open, burps loudly and grabs the food. Which word describes him? | calm | *uncouth | polite | gentle
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to uncouth? | ancient | happy | *rude | kind
test: Is uncouth about how someone behaves or how much money they have? | What colour their coat is | How tall they are | *How someone behaves | How much money they have
test: In The Wind in the Willows, who are the uncouth guests in Toad Hall? | Moles and ducks | Owls and mice | Badgers and rabbits | *Weasels and stoats
exam: Sam says uncouth is just another word for poor. Is he right? | Yes, because poor people are always rude | Yes, uncouth and poor mean exactly the same | No, uncouth means very clever | *No, uncouth describes rude, rough behaviour, not how much money someone has
